WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.297 --> 00:00:06.380
IL-SUNG LEE: Hi, everyone.

00:00:06.380 --> 00:00:08.830
My name is Il-Sung Lee, and
I'm a senior product manager

00:00:08.830 --> 00:00:11.230
for Google Cloud working
on key management

00:00:11.230 --> 00:00:14.050
and obviously,
Cloud HSM With me, I

00:00:14.050 --> 00:00:17.740
have Adit Sinha, who's one of
our tech leads for Cloud HSM,

00:00:17.740 --> 00:00:20.080
to talk about Cloud
HSM and give you

00:00:20.080 --> 00:00:24.220
a deep dive and best practices
around the service itself.

00:00:24.220 --> 00:00:28.090
In this session, we'll talk
first about the HSM overview

00:00:28.090 --> 00:00:30.890
and give you an idea
of what Cloud HSM is

00:00:30.890 --> 00:00:32.500
and what it can do
for you, and then

00:00:32.500 --> 00:00:35.868
highlight some of the key
features for Cloud HSM.

00:00:35.868 --> 00:00:37.910
Then I'll turn it over to
Adit, who will actually

00:00:37.910 --> 00:00:40.420
talk more about the
overall architecture

00:00:40.420 --> 00:00:43.310
and talk about some of the
key aspects of architecture,

00:00:43.310 --> 00:00:47.890
such as tenant separation and
Google administrative access.

00:00:47.890 --> 00:00:50.410
So start off with, let's
go and talk a little bit

00:00:50.410 --> 00:00:53.230
about the overview of Cloud HSM.

00:00:53.230 --> 00:00:55.760
That picture there is
actually a real picture of one

00:00:55.760 --> 00:00:58.870
the cards that we have
inside our data centers.

00:00:58.870 --> 00:01:02.080
This is a Cavium
LiquidSecurity HSM.

00:01:02.080 --> 00:01:04.690
That black stuff you
see all around it

00:01:04.690 --> 00:01:07.000
is actually epoxy
that's used to provide

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.870
tamper evidence and tamper
resistance for the card itself.

00:01:10.870 --> 00:01:13.090
And it's one of the
requirements for the card

00:01:13.090 --> 00:01:17.050
to be a FIPS 140-2 card, and
I'll explain a little bit more

00:01:17.050 --> 00:01:19.140
about that in a bit.

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.790
But the Cloud's HSM
service is basically--

00:01:22.790 --> 00:01:24.900
HSM is a managed service.

00:01:24.900 --> 00:01:26.903
And so, if you're not
familiar with HSMs,

00:01:26.903 --> 00:01:28.320
they're hardware
security modules.

00:01:28.320 --> 00:01:31.870
They're basically industry
devices that are used--

00:01:31.870 --> 00:01:34.230
hardware devices that are
used to actually protect

00:01:34.230 --> 00:01:38.040
generated key material and
also perform crypto operations.

00:01:38.040 --> 00:01:41.250
Now, the Cloud HSM
that we provide

00:01:41.250 --> 00:01:44.490
is actually a fully managed
service that Google provides,

00:01:44.490 --> 00:01:46.220
as you can see in
the diagram there.

00:01:46.220 --> 00:01:47.800
A request comes in.

00:01:47.800 --> 00:01:50.968
It goes through the key
management service front end.

00:01:50.968 --> 00:01:53.010
And then based upon the
type of key your created,

00:01:53.010 --> 00:01:55.770
it either goes to a software
path or the hardware path.

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:58.950
If it's in the hardware
path, our Cloud HSM servers

00:01:58.950 --> 00:02:01.500
will orchestrate the
operations to occur within one

00:02:01.500 --> 00:02:04.320
of the actual physical HSMs.

00:02:04.320 --> 00:02:07.590
So the architecture
actually lends itself

00:02:07.590 --> 00:02:09.060
to being a full cloud service.

00:02:09.060 --> 00:02:12.560
And I won't talk too much about
this, because in a few slides,

00:02:12.560 --> 00:02:14.455
Adit will talk more about this.

00:02:14.455 --> 00:02:16.080
But what I mean is
that we'll take care

00:02:16.080 --> 00:02:17.340
of all the patching for you.

00:02:17.340 --> 00:02:18.952
So you don't have
to worry about any

00:02:18.952 --> 00:02:20.910
of the physical maintenance,
firmware, upgrades

00:02:20.910 --> 00:02:22.410
or anything else like that.

00:02:22.410 --> 00:02:23.970
It's all done
without any downtime.

00:02:23.970 --> 00:02:25.620
So it doesn't really make a--

00:02:25.620 --> 00:02:27.840
it doesn't really impact
you from an availability

00:02:27.840 --> 00:02:29.370
perspective.

00:02:29.370 --> 00:02:33.540
We also auto scale on this,
in terms of requests as well.

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.870
If you have a lot of
requests, then our service

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:42.150
will just be able to fulfill
all those requests as well

00:02:42.150 --> 00:02:45.125
within the limits of your quota.

00:02:45.125 --> 00:02:46.500
The other thing
is that you don't

00:02:46.500 --> 00:02:49.190
have to worry about backups
and having a kind of business

00:02:49.190 --> 00:02:51.690
continuity strategy, because
we'll take care of that for you

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:52.580
as well.

00:02:52.580 --> 00:02:54.360
So we'll take care
of the backups.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.810
We have a very, very,
highly redundant system

00:02:56.810 --> 00:02:59.790
that'll make sure that your
key material is very secure,

00:02:59.790 --> 00:03:01.800
and that the chances
of losing your keys

00:03:01.800 --> 00:03:08.130
are actually extremely small
to almost complete unlikely.

00:03:08.130 --> 00:03:10.080
The other thing about
the HSMs as well

00:03:10.080 --> 00:03:12.570
is that they're all contained
within our very secure

00:03:12.570 --> 00:03:14.400
facilities.

00:03:14.400 --> 00:03:19.200
And so they're-- basically
inherit all the physically

00:03:19.200 --> 00:03:23.160
securities of all our servers
and all the other devices have

00:03:23.160 --> 00:03:25.740
within our data centers.

00:03:25.740 --> 00:03:30.090
And they basically provides
a level of security

00:03:30.090 --> 00:03:33.235
that will be pretty hard to
impersonate outside of a cloud

00:03:33.235 --> 00:03:33.735
provider.

00:03:36.290 --> 00:03:38.120
Why would you use Cloud HSM?

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:39.520
There's a number of reasons.

00:03:39.520 --> 00:03:42.530
So number one, it's
typically compliance.

00:03:42.530 --> 00:03:47.420
So a lot of people use Cloud
HSM, or HSMs in general,

00:03:47.420 --> 00:03:50.870
because they're looking for
a certification from NIST.

00:03:50.870 --> 00:03:54.320
It's essentially
FIPS 140-2 level 3

00:03:54.320 --> 00:03:57.290
that our HSMs are
currently running in.

00:03:57.290 --> 00:04:01.550
Now, what that means is that the
FIPS 140-2 is a crypto standard

00:04:01.550 --> 00:04:03.440
devised by NIST again.

00:04:03.440 --> 00:04:05.930
And it defines
things like what--

00:04:05.930 --> 00:04:10.200
random number generations,
the type of algorithms,

00:04:10.200 --> 00:04:12.470
key types, things like that.

00:04:12.470 --> 00:04:16.510
The level 3 is basically only
attainable through hardware

00:04:16.510 --> 00:04:17.170
devices.

00:04:17.170 --> 00:04:22.150
And it dictates things like
the physical tamper evidence

00:04:22.150 --> 00:04:24.610
and tamper proofness
of the device itself,

00:04:24.610 --> 00:04:27.400
so that you can't get
in there and physically

00:04:27.400 --> 00:04:30.890
try to extract the keys, or
it's very difficult to do so.

00:04:30.890 --> 00:04:33.220
Regionalization is
also another key aspect

00:04:33.220 --> 00:04:36.070
of the service where, when
you actually create a key

00:04:36.070 --> 00:04:37.590
within a specific region--

00:04:37.590 --> 00:04:42.220
let's say that it's
us-central1, then that key only

00:04:42.220 --> 00:04:45.070
exists within us-central1.

00:04:45.070 --> 00:04:48.310
And not only the key
but also the metadata,

00:04:48.310 --> 00:04:50.600
all the backups, everything
else associated with that.

00:04:50.600 --> 00:04:53.920
So wherever you create the
key, that's where it'll be.

00:04:53.920 --> 00:04:57.110
Now, Cloud HSM also
provides an additional level

00:04:57.110 --> 00:04:59.960
of regional affinity
based on the fact

00:04:59.960 --> 00:05:02.330
that, when you
create the keys, it's

00:05:02.330 --> 00:05:05.600
associated with a set of
HSMs that are physically

00:05:05.600 --> 00:05:06.610
within that region.

00:05:06.610 --> 00:05:08.960
If that key was ever--

00:05:08.960 --> 00:05:10.780
if you ever tried
to actually decrypt

00:05:10.780 --> 00:05:13.210
that key in a different
region, it's not possible.

00:05:13.210 --> 00:05:15.710
You will understand what I'm
talking about a little bit more

00:05:15.710 --> 00:05:17.340
when Adit talks about
the architecture,

00:05:17.340 --> 00:05:20.180
but essentially that
key can only ever be

00:05:20.180 --> 00:05:22.840
used by those devices
within that region.

00:05:22.840 --> 00:05:25.550
And then finally, the last
thing, and I'll just reiterate,

00:05:25.550 --> 00:05:30.170
is that it leverages the
exact same APIs as Cloud KMS.

00:05:30.170 --> 00:05:32.690
What that really means
is that, if you already

00:05:32.690 --> 00:05:36.350
know how to use Cloud KMS, then
you know how to use Cloud HSM.

00:05:36.350 --> 00:05:38.430
Any application
that uses Cloud KMS

00:05:38.430 --> 00:05:41.960
can use Cloud HSM, including
all the internal integrations

00:05:41.960 --> 00:05:42.930
that we have as well.

00:05:42.930 --> 00:05:45.910
So all the servers that are
integrated with our Cloud KMS

00:05:45.910 --> 00:05:49.740
will be able to use HSMs
without any kind of changes.

00:05:49.740 --> 00:05:52.407
It's only when you create the
key you specify protection

00:05:52.407 --> 00:05:53.240
level and that's it.

00:05:56.340 --> 00:05:58.980
Now, talking in terms
of key features,

00:05:58.980 --> 00:06:02.220
there are three that I
like to actually cover

00:06:02.220 --> 00:06:04.170
in a little more detail.

00:06:04.170 --> 00:06:06.300
Those are the global
multi-regional support,

00:06:06.300 --> 00:06:09.860
the attestation statements,
and the CMEK integration.

00:06:09.860 --> 00:06:13.310
CMEK stands for Customer-Managed
Encryption Keys.

00:06:13.310 --> 00:06:17.720
In terms of the global
multi-regional support, first

00:06:17.720 --> 00:06:21.860
of all, we have Cloud HSM
available in all our cloud

00:06:21.860 --> 00:06:22.860
regions.

00:06:22.860 --> 00:06:25.010
So any region that
you choose today

00:06:25.010 --> 00:06:28.480
should have Cloud HSM
available with it as well.

00:06:28.480 --> 00:06:30.890
It has a large footprint.

00:06:30.890 --> 00:06:35.810
It's probably one of the largest
footprints for HSM service

00:06:35.810 --> 00:06:39.180
that you'll find anywhere,
to be quite honest.

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:41.990
The other thing is
that it will be--

00:06:41.990 --> 00:06:43.910
you have the capability
of actually creating

00:06:43.910 --> 00:06:46.970
a multi-region with
our Cloud HSM keys.

00:06:46.970 --> 00:06:48.380
What that means
is that, when you

00:06:48.380 --> 00:06:50.900
create a key in a
multi-region, it's

00:06:50.900 --> 00:06:53.890
available across that geography.

00:06:53.890 --> 00:06:56.830
For example, if you create
a key within the US region,

00:06:56.830 --> 00:07:01.428
then that key is accessible
through different areas

00:07:01.428 --> 00:07:02.470
within the United States.

00:07:02.470 --> 00:07:05.260
It's available
across the country,

00:07:05.260 --> 00:07:09.070
so that if for whatever reason
your particular region goes

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:11.320
dark, which hopefully
never happens,

00:07:11.320 --> 00:07:15.580
then that key exists in
other areas and other regions

00:07:15.580 --> 00:07:18.680
or other parts of the country,
so that your application can

00:07:18.680 --> 00:07:21.130
migrate over to a different
part of the country

00:07:21.130 --> 00:07:22.540
and the key will just be there.

00:07:22.540 --> 00:07:24.340
It'll be completely available.

00:07:24.340 --> 00:07:27.410
When you make changes to
a key in a multi-region,

00:07:27.410 --> 00:07:29.510
the changes are
reflected everywhere.

00:07:29.510 --> 00:07:32.440
So it happens almost
instantaneously.

00:07:32.440 --> 00:07:35.080
So it's truly a multi-region.

00:07:35.080 --> 00:07:37.930
It's not a backup and restore.

00:07:37.930 --> 00:07:40.330
In other schemes,
you may see systems

00:07:40.330 --> 00:07:43.150
where you actually are
taking your backup,

00:07:43.150 --> 00:07:45.430
and restoring, and cloning
it to a different region,

00:07:45.430 --> 00:07:49.210
so that you basically
mimic having two regions

00:07:49.210 --> 00:07:52.090
and having a business
continuity solution.

00:07:52.090 --> 00:07:54.190
Ours is a little bit
more automated than that.

00:07:54.190 --> 00:07:56.300
Just define your key to
be in a multi-region,

00:07:56.300 --> 00:07:58.750
and it does exists there.

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:01.480
We recently, also,
announced support

00:08:01.480 --> 00:08:05.020
for a global, regional
support, which

00:08:05.020 --> 00:08:07.930
means that if you create a
key within a global region,

00:08:07.930 --> 00:08:10.000
that key becomes
available across--

00:08:10.000 --> 00:08:11.210
around the globe.

00:08:11.210 --> 00:08:13.840
So a key that's created, for
example, in the United States

00:08:13.840 --> 00:08:17.770
may be also available in
India, or also available

00:08:17.770 --> 00:08:22.120
across Europe if you
create it as a global key.

00:08:22.120 --> 00:08:25.470
And for anyone who's actually
worked directly with HSMs,

00:08:25.470 --> 00:08:29.740
you know that that is a pretty
special thing to be able to do,

00:08:29.740 --> 00:08:31.607
because creating
clusters with HSMs

00:08:31.607 --> 00:08:33.190
is not that simple,
especially when it

00:08:33.190 --> 00:08:35.470
spans massive geographies.

00:08:35.470 --> 00:08:37.990
Because of the
architecture that we

00:08:37.990 --> 00:08:40.929
chose that Adit
will explain in bit,

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:43.330
you'll see why we can
actually do things

00:08:43.330 --> 00:08:48.020
like this, which makes it
truly like a cloud service.

00:08:48.020 --> 00:08:52.130
The second thing I want to
cover is attestation statements.

00:08:52.130 --> 00:08:53.565
What an attestation
statement is,

00:08:53.565 --> 00:08:57.200
is that it's a statement
that's signed by the HSM

00:08:57.200 --> 00:08:59.750
using a certificate
by the HSM vendor,

00:08:59.750 --> 00:09:04.190
that attests to the
key and the properties

00:09:04.190 --> 00:09:06.650
around the keys
being inside the HSM.

00:09:06.650 --> 00:09:10.308
So if you create a
key using our APIs,

00:09:10.308 --> 00:09:12.350
then you can download that
attestation statement,

00:09:12.350 --> 00:09:16.060
and verify the signature, and
know that the key is definitely

00:09:16.060 --> 00:09:16.820
inside--

00:09:16.820 --> 00:09:19.023
created by one of our HSMs.

00:09:19.023 --> 00:09:20.690
And when you perform
a crypto operation,

00:09:20.690 --> 00:09:23.280
it will happen within
the HSM itself.

00:09:23.280 --> 00:09:26.480
Now, the other things you can do
with the attestation statement

00:09:26.480 --> 00:09:29.390
is that you can look at the
various properties of the keys,

00:09:29.390 --> 00:09:32.850
including things like whether
the key is extract or not.

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:36.260
So you can actually get
verifiable proof that that key

00:09:36.260 --> 00:09:39.360
material cannot be taken
out HSM in plaintext,

00:09:39.360 --> 00:09:41.293
and that Google
can't do this either.

00:09:41.293 --> 00:09:42.710
This is something
that'll probably

00:09:42.710 --> 00:09:45.090
make your auditors very happy.

00:09:45.090 --> 00:09:47.510
Other things that
you can do are look

00:09:47.510 --> 00:09:48.962
at the different properties.

00:09:48.962 --> 00:09:50.420
One of the more
interesting ones is

00:09:50.420 --> 00:09:54.000
that if you actually are
using a asymmetric key,

00:09:54.000 --> 00:09:57.620
you can actually download the
SHA-256 hash of the public key

00:09:57.620 --> 00:09:58.290
itself.

00:09:58.290 --> 00:10:01.250
So when you download
a public key from HSM,

00:10:01.250 --> 00:10:03.222
you can take those
SHA-256 hash, compare it

00:10:03.222 --> 00:10:05.180
to the one that's in the
attestation statement,

00:10:05.180 --> 00:10:08.600
and if they match, then
you know that you're

00:10:08.600 --> 00:10:11.540
talking to the right
key and that you

00:10:11.540 --> 00:10:13.040
have cryptographic
proof that you're

00:10:13.040 --> 00:10:14.440
talking to a key within HSM.

00:10:17.480 --> 00:10:20.960
The final thing I'll talk about
is customer-managed encryption

00:10:20.960 --> 00:10:23.450
keys support services.

00:10:23.450 --> 00:10:25.250
All the services
that you see here

00:10:25.250 --> 00:10:28.010
are already integrated
with our Cloud KMS.

00:10:28.010 --> 00:10:32.150
There's nine of them listed
in the integrated services.

00:10:32.150 --> 00:10:33.740
On the virtue of
the fact that we've

00:10:33.740 --> 00:10:37.550
designed Cloud HSM to
leverage the exact same API,

00:10:37.550 --> 00:10:40.760
it means that anywhere
where Cloud KMS we use,

00:10:40.760 --> 00:10:43.350
you can actually leverage
Cloud HSM as well.

00:10:43.350 --> 00:10:44.750
The only difference
is, again, is

00:10:44.750 --> 00:10:46.292
that when you create
the key, there's

00:10:46.292 --> 00:10:48.080
a different flag that you set.

00:10:48.080 --> 00:10:50.720
So, it's probably one of the
fastest experiences you'll ever

00:10:50.720 --> 00:10:53.910
have in creating a key in
HSM Then with that key,

00:10:53.910 --> 00:10:55.980
you can actually use it
to protect your data in,

00:10:55.980 --> 00:10:59.360
for example Cloud SQL,
or GCS, or BigQuery,

00:10:59.360 --> 00:11:01.490
or any of the
services listed there.

00:11:01.490 --> 00:11:03.690
The CMEK compliant
services are services

00:11:03.690 --> 00:11:08.430
that don't persist data in any
significant length of time,

00:11:08.430 --> 00:11:10.400
which is why they're
CMEK compliant.

00:11:10.400 --> 00:11:11.990
If you look at the
URL that I have

00:11:11.990 --> 00:11:14.840
at the bottom of
the slide, this page

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:17.330
will be constantly updated
with new services that we add,

00:11:17.330 --> 00:11:19.550
because we're
constantly trying to add

00:11:19.550 --> 00:11:23.540
more services to the support
list for Cloud KMS and Cloud

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.330
HSM.

00:11:25.330 --> 00:11:28.810
With that, I'm going to
add three more things

00:11:28.810 --> 00:11:32.490
I want to actually talk about.

00:11:32.490 --> 00:11:37.350
First, customers pay for
key operations not capacity.

00:11:37.350 --> 00:11:39.960
What we're saying here is
that Cloud HSM is actually

00:11:39.960 --> 00:11:41.880
a consumption based model.

00:11:41.880 --> 00:11:44.025
You don't have to
basically purchase

00:11:44.025 --> 00:11:47.340
an entire partition, or
partitions, or clusters

00:11:47.340 --> 00:11:48.450
of partitions.

00:11:48.450 --> 00:11:50.850
You basically pay for
every crypto operation.

00:11:50.850 --> 00:11:53.550
Every key that you create.

00:11:53.550 --> 00:11:57.300
I believe right now, it's $0.03
per 10,000 operations for most

00:11:57.300 --> 00:12:01.920
type of operations, and it's
about $1.00 a month per key.

00:12:01.920 --> 00:12:03.460
You can use as much as you want.

00:12:03.460 --> 00:12:05.680
You can use as
little as you like.

00:12:05.680 --> 00:12:08.670
It's bascially, there's
no upfront cost to this,

00:12:08.670 --> 00:12:10.810
and there's no commitment.

00:12:10.810 --> 00:12:14.100
There's no additional cost
for multi-regional operations.

00:12:14.100 --> 00:12:18.680
Despite the fact that this
key can span multiple--

00:12:18.680 --> 00:12:21.610
a vast geography, we don't
charge you any extra for that.

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:24.810
It's bascially the same
cost I just quoted above.

00:12:24.810 --> 00:12:26.790
Finally, the high
availability is

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:30.420
inherent as a property
of the service itself.

00:12:30.420 --> 00:12:34.530
There's no requirement for you
to actually take backups, store

00:12:34.530 --> 00:12:37.470
the backups, or do
anything else like that.

00:12:37.470 --> 00:12:39.180
We have a very,
very robust system

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:41.310
that takes very, very
frequent backups,

00:12:41.310 --> 00:12:45.153
and takes the key
material and actually

00:12:45.153 --> 00:12:47.070
backs them up into a
disparate system as well,

00:12:47.070 --> 00:12:53.160
so that we are resilient against
failures of any particular data

00:12:53.160 --> 00:12:55.303
store.

00:12:55.303 --> 00:12:56.970
With that, now I'm
going to turn it over

00:12:56.970 --> 00:12:58.887
to Adit, who's going to
talk a little bit more

00:12:58.887 --> 00:13:02.590
about the architectural view.

00:13:02.590 --> 00:13:04.160
ADIT SINHA: Thanks, Il-Sung.

00:13:04.160 --> 00:13:06.850
Like Il-Sung said, I'll go
into more details about how

00:13:06.850 --> 00:13:08.950
the architecture
works, and hopefully

00:13:08.950 --> 00:13:11.710
make it clear how all the
features that Il-Sung talked

00:13:11.710 --> 00:13:13.950
about fall out of our design.

00:13:13.950 --> 00:13:16.660
I'll go more into detail about
a lot of different aspects.

00:13:16.660 --> 00:13:19.330
But to start with, I
want to talk about some

00:13:19.330 --> 00:13:22.460
of the design principles that
we have behind our system.

00:13:22.460 --> 00:13:26.800
First, we want to
reduce user API access.

00:13:26.800 --> 00:13:29.920
HSMs are inherently
complex devices

00:13:29.920 --> 00:13:32.380
and are similar to black boxes.

00:13:32.380 --> 00:13:34.870
In order to try to
prevent customers

00:13:34.870 --> 00:13:38.770
from launching attacks against
the HSMs and the service,

00:13:38.770 --> 00:13:41.110
we want to restrict
the types of operations

00:13:41.110 --> 00:13:43.110
they're allowed to do.

00:13:43.110 --> 00:13:47.010
Second, one of the major
reasons to move to the cloud

00:13:47.010 --> 00:13:50.400
is to no longer need to
manage your own devices.

00:13:50.400 --> 00:13:55.350
We want Cloud HSM to fit in
that same customer experience.

00:13:55.350 --> 00:13:58.020
As Il-Sung said,
we want customers

00:13:58.020 --> 00:14:00.030
to pay for
consumption model, not

00:14:00.030 --> 00:14:04.200
for having to provision
their resources beforehand.

00:14:04.200 --> 00:14:08.130
And third, a lot of customers
have compliance requirements

00:14:08.130 --> 00:14:10.920
around where their keys
are geographically located.

00:14:10.920 --> 00:14:14.520
We want strong cryptographic
and administrative controls

00:14:14.520 --> 00:14:19.190
to make sure that we can
maintain these requirements.

00:14:19.190 --> 00:14:22.540
The system that we've
designed for these principles

00:14:22.540 --> 00:14:25.840
looks slightly different than a
normal HSM in the cloud model.

00:14:25.840 --> 00:14:30.550
In the traditional model, you
would have crypto op access

00:14:30.550 --> 00:14:33.310
represented by the
blue arrows and admin

00:14:33.310 --> 00:14:36.230
access represented
by the red arrows

00:14:36.230 --> 00:14:38.830
to your own partition on HSM.

00:14:38.830 --> 00:14:41.890
The cloud provider would have
admin access to the device

00:14:41.890 --> 00:14:46.000
as a whole, which they would
use for operations like setting

00:14:46.000 --> 00:14:49.080
the HSM up initially for you.

00:14:49.080 --> 00:14:54.570
In contrast, in Cloud HSM,
you only get crypto op access.

00:14:54.570 --> 00:14:58.830
That access is to shared
partitions on the HSM,

00:14:58.830 --> 00:15:03.240
and it's mediated by
the Cloud HSM service.

00:15:03.240 --> 00:15:08.040
Google retains admin access to
both the partitions and the HSM

00:15:08.040 --> 00:15:09.030
device.

00:15:09.030 --> 00:15:12.240
The Cloud HSM service
is responsible for load

00:15:12.240 --> 00:15:16.800
balancing and controlling
access to the HSM.

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.480
It can see plain texts,
but can't see key material.

00:15:21.480 --> 00:15:24.600
Since these are fixed
devices, raw key material

00:15:24.600 --> 00:15:27.580
must remain on the HSM.

00:15:27.580 --> 00:15:31.360
Instead, the service can see
wrapped key material, which

00:15:31.360 --> 00:15:36.520
uses extensively in the
system to make it scalable

00:15:36.520 --> 00:15:37.840
and highly available.

00:15:37.840 --> 00:15:40.900
Each region has a
wrapping key that's

00:15:40.900 --> 00:15:44.960
replicated to a partition on
all of the HSMs in the region.

00:15:44.960 --> 00:15:47.410
When you create a
key, the service

00:15:47.410 --> 00:15:51.280
wraps your key with the
regions wrapping key

00:15:51.280 --> 00:15:53.260
and collects the attestation.

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:57.040
It puts the wrapped key
in an external database.

00:15:57.040 --> 00:15:59.380
When you want to do
a crypto operation,

00:15:59.380 --> 00:16:02.530
the service will retrieve the
wrapped key from the database

00:16:02.530 --> 00:16:05.365
and send it to an HSM
along with the inputs

00:16:05.365 --> 00:16:07.000
to the crypto operation--

00:16:07.000 --> 00:16:10.420
for example, with the
plaintext in an encryption.

00:16:10.420 --> 00:16:13.750
The HSM will unwrap the
key and do the operation.

00:16:13.750 --> 00:16:15.790
This scheme has a few benefits.

00:16:15.790 --> 00:16:20.710
First, as soon as your key is
created, any HSM in the region

00:16:20.710 --> 00:16:23.710
can use it immediately.

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:26.220
There is no
synchronization mechanism

00:16:26.220 --> 00:16:29.430
that needs to happen online.

00:16:29.430 --> 00:16:34.850
Second, the durability of your
keys isn't tied to the HSMs.

00:16:34.850 --> 00:16:37.700
Your keys are all backed
up in an external database.

00:16:37.700 --> 00:16:39.980
And third, regardless
of the number

00:16:39.980 --> 00:16:42.290
of operations that you
have or the number of keys

00:16:42.290 --> 00:16:46.640
that you have, all customers
experience the availability

00:16:46.640 --> 00:16:50.120
of being backed by a full
region's worth of HSMs.

00:16:50.120 --> 00:16:52.040
In order to scale
up in this model,

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:57.470
we'll install a new HSM in
the data center for the region

00:16:57.470 --> 00:17:01.900
and clone the region's
wrapping key to that new HSM.

00:17:01.900 --> 00:17:03.790
Additionally, if the
region is supposed

00:17:03.790 --> 00:17:07.119
to support a multi-region, we'll
clone the multi-region wrapping

00:17:07.119 --> 00:17:09.640
key to a separate partition.

00:17:09.640 --> 00:17:13.579
For example, here we
have three cloud regions,

00:17:13.579 --> 00:17:18.940
the first two of which also
support the same multi-region.

00:17:18.940 --> 00:17:21.550
The HSMs in the first
two cloud regions

00:17:21.550 --> 00:17:25.000
have a second partition
that is cloned

00:17:25.000 --> 00:17:27.640
with the same
multi-regional wrapping key.

00:17:27.640 --> 00:17:30.910
The HSMs in cloud region 3
don't have the second partition,

00:17:30.910 --> 00:17:34.220
because the region doesn't
support the multi-region.

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:38.120
When you create a key
in a multi-region,

00:17:38.120 --> 00:17:41.460
the process looks very
similar to that as a region.

00:17:41.460 --> 00:17:44.360
The Cloud HSM service creates
the key in the partition

00:17:44.360 --> 00:17:45.750
and wraps it.

00:17:45.750 --> 00:17:49.140
This means that as soon as you
create a key in a multi-region,

00:17:49.140 --> 00:17:52.850
it can be used immediately
anywhere in that multi-region.

00:17:52.850 --> 00:17:55.370
We bootstrap off
the rest of Google's

00:17:55.370 --> 00:17:56.780
synchronized databases.

00:17:56.780 --> 00:18:00.050
Also, by using
separate wrapping keys

00:18:00.050 --> 00:18:03.680
for different regions and
different multi-regions,

00:18:03.680 --> 00:18:07.440
the service ensures that
keys can't be used outside

00:18:07.440 --> 00:18:09.540
of their intended region.

00:18:09.540 --> 00:18:14.510
For example, a key wrapped
with the region 1 wrapping

00:18:14.510 --> 00:18:18.050
key can't be used
in cloud region 2,

00:18:18.050 --> 00:18:22.060
because none of the HSMs in
region 2 can load that key.

00:18:22.060 --> 00:18:25.660
Likewise, a key wrapped with
the multi-regional wrapping key

00:18:25.660 --> 00:18:29.720
can't be used in cloud
region 3 for the same reason.

00:18:29.720 --> 00:18:32.810
This ensures that once
we've set the system up,

00:18:32.810 --> 00:18:36.410
it automatically enforces
regionalization constraints.

00:18:36.410 --> 00:18:40.520
Setting the system up, that
is cloning the wrapping keys

00:18:40.520 --> 00:18:44.360
across different HSMs, is
an administrative operation.

00:18:44.360 --> 00:18:47.000
We have a different set
of controls to make sure

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:48.360
that happens safely.

00:18:48.360 --> 00:18:50.950
First, only a small
number of Googlers

00:18:50.950 --> 00:18:55.640
have permission to
perform admin operations.

00:18:55.640 --> 00:19:00.350
The operations require M-of-N
authorization on the HSMs.

00:19:00.350 --> 00:19:02.360
So the HSMs require
multiple Googlers

00:19:02.360 --> 00:19:05.950
to authorize each
operation, and Googlers

00:19:05.950 --> 00:19:08.120
need to provide two-factor
authentication in order

00:19:08.120 --> 00:19:09.020
to do so.

00:19:09.020 --> 00:19:13.730
All of the operations happen in
a secure and highly restricted

00:19:13.730 --> 00:19:15.830
access facility.

00:19:15.830 --> 00:19:21.320
We have extensive audit logs
both for access to the facility

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:23.540
and all of the operations
that happen inside.

00:19:23.540 --> 00:19:25.430
Additionally, as
Il-Sung mentioned

00:19:25.430 --> 00:19:27.770
that these are
fixed devices, they

00:19:27.770 --> 00:19:30.260
don't allow specific
operations regardless

00:19:30.260 --> 00:19:32.940
of who is doing them,
even HSM administrator.

00:19:32.940 --> 00:19:37.310
For example, you can't
export plaintext key material

00:19:37.310 --> 00:19:40.370
of either customer
keys or wrapping keys.

00:19:40.370 --> 00:19:45.630
And finally, many of our
systems are built in such a way

00:19:45.630 --> 00:19:50.610
to not allow HSM
administrators to perform

00:19:50.610 --> 00:19:54.240
crypto operations even with
administrative credentials.

00:19:54.240 --> 00:19:56.220
We have all these
mechanisms in place

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:59.010
to keep you safe from Googlers.

00:19:59.010 --> 00:20:01.360
Other customers,
on the other hand,

00:20:01.360 --> 00:20:03.660
only have crypto
operation access.

00:20:03.660 --> 00:20:05.760
So to keep you
safe from them, we

00:20:05.760 --> 00:20:07.710
have a different
set of mechanisms.

00:20:07.710 --> 00:20:10.530
First, when a customer
does an operation,

00:20:10.530 --> 00:20:12.810
we check whether they
have permission to do

00:20:12.810 --> 00:20:15.540
the operation in Cloud IAM.

00:20:15.540 --> 00:20:19.620
By using Cloud IAM, you
can use the same set

00:20:19.620 --> 00:20:23.010
of permissions and
policies that you

00:20:23.010 --> 00:20:25.440
use to manage the rest
of your cloud resources

00:20:25.440 --> 00:20:27.840
to also manage your HSM keys.

00:20:27.840 --> 00:20:31.490
Second, we check that the
customer has sufficient quota

00:20:31.490 --> 00:20:33.380
to do the operation.

00:20:33.380 --> 00:20:36.620
In order to make sure that
we have enough resources

00:20:36.620 --> 00:20:40.830
to serve requests for your
workloads when you need them,

00:20:40.830 --> 00:20:43.580
we need to make sure that other
customers aren't unexpectedly

00:20:43.580 --> 00:20:45.050
overloading the service.

00:20:45.050 --> 00:20:48.470
And third, we have basic request
validation before the request

00:20:48.470 --> 00:20:49.760
reaches the HSM.

00:20:49.760 --> 00:20:52.970
For example, we check
that different arguments

00:20:52.970 --> 00:20:56.270
are within their
expected range in size

00:20:56.270 --> 00:20:57.920
and also the key
types are correct.

00:20:57.920 --> 00:21:02.180
This, coupled with the fact
that there is no customer admin

00:21:02.180 --> 00:21:07.730
access, significantly reduces
the attacks surface available,

00:21:07.730 --> 00:21:09.380
creating a safer product.

00:21:09.380 --> 00:21:13.400
In summary, we've covered quite
a few details of about how

00:21:13.400 --> 00:21:16.350
the Cloud HSM service works.

00:21:16.350 --> 00:21:18.230
We've seen how we
use key wrapping

00:21:18.230 --> 00:21:23.480
and partitions to make a highly
available and scalable service

00:21:23.480 --> 00:21:28.440
that takes away the need for you
to manage your own HSM devices.

00:21:28.440 --> 00:21:33.090
We've seen how this system keeps
you safe from both Googlers

00:21:33.090 --> 00:21:36.690
and from other customers through
our administrative controls

00:21:36.690 --> 00:21:39.390
and tenant isolation.

00:21:39.390 --> 00:21:42.600
We've seen how you
can easily create keys

00:21:42.600 --> 00:21:45.180
in regions and multi-regions.

00:21:45.180 --> 00:21:48.270
The consequence of all
these design decisions

00:21:48.270 --> 00:21:51.150
is that it is as easy
to protect your data,

00:21:51.150 --> 00:21:54.960
both in Google
services and directly

00:21:54.960 --> 00:21:57.060
as it is with a software key.

00:21:57.060 --> 00:22:00.740
We'll have all of these details
and more in an upcoming Cloud

00:22:00.740 --> 00:22:01.960
HSM white paper.

00:22:01.960 --> 00:22:03.630
So please keep your
eye out for that.

00:22:03.630 --> 00:22:05.420
Thank you.

